      "code_sha256_lf": "2267c279b194cd15354ba6c9265276468439e0cc14b2543149afd551139dd350",
      "rationale": "The regression's single root cause is the typo `r'\\s+?'` (lazy quantifier) for `tokens.Whitespace` in keywords.SQL_REGEX: because the lexer uses re.match, a lazy `+?` always matches just one character, so every run of 2+ plain whitespace characters is split into that many separate one-character Whitespace tokens instead of a single token. Filters such as StripWhitespaceFilter._stripws_identifierlist (documented for issue140 as removing all whitespace/newlines before a comma) only remove the single token adjacent to the comma, so with the fragmented tokens the rest of the run leaks through, producing wrong output (\"a , b\" instead of \"a, b\") \u2014 this is exactly the class of formatting/reindent regressions described in the report. The first test pins down the root cause directly (a run of spaces must lex as one token, consistent with every other greedy rule in SQL_REGEX and with how the rest of the codebase already assumes whitespace runs are single tokens); the next two tests check the user-visible, documented behavior (format/reindent must fully strip whitespace before commas) that breaks as a direct consequence, and which is restored once the regex is fixed to `r'\\s+'`.",
